  <p>The 1080 by 1920 figure is arithmetic. YouTube writes "1080p"; a 9:16 frame at 1080p is 1080 pixels wide and 1920 tall. The encoding and file-cap rows cover every YouTube upload, Shorts included, not Shorts alone.</p>

  <h2>What makes YouTube treat an upload as a Short?</h2>

  <p>Two things, the shape and the running time. YouTube's upload page lists no other condition and no separate button: a square or vertical file of three minutes or less goes in through the ordinary upload.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">YouTube categorizes any video uploaded on or after October 15, 2024 with a square or vertical aspect ratio, up to three minutes long, as a Short. Videos uploaded before that date stay long-form videos.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: YouTube Help, "Understand three-minute YouTube Shorts," read October 2026, platform's own help text. https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/15424877</p>

  <p>Plenty of guides still give 60 seconds. YouTube's own pages say three minutes, a change announced on October 3, 2024, for "videos that are square or taller in aspect ratio." A square 1:1 video counts. A 16:9 video never does, however short.</p>

  <p>The same help page carries the rule most guides skip, and its English and French versions don't agree on it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Any Short that is over one minute in duration with an active Content ID claim of any type, including manual claims, will be blocked globally on YouTube." A blocked Short can't be played, recommended or monetized.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: YouTube Help, "Understand three-minute YouTube Shorts," English version, undated, read October 2026, platform's own help text. https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/15424877</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">From September 24, 2026, new Shorts longer than one minute but under three minutes with an active Content ID claim are no longer blocked automatically and can stay available on YouTube. Shorts blocked worldwide still can't be monetized.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: YouTube Help, French version of the same page, read October 2026, platform's own help text, our translation. https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/15424877?hl=fr</p>

  <p>The English page carries no date, the French one does, so both are given here until they match. Under either, clear the music rights before you post: blocked or not, a Short with a claim can lose its monetization until the claim is resolved. Songs picked from the Shorts audio library are a different case: most can play for up to 90 seconds in a three-minute Short, though some tracks stop at 60 or 30.</p>

  <h2>How do you upload a Short from a computer or a phone?</h2>

  <p>There are three ways in. On a phone, the Shorts camera in the YouTube app records one or more clips that add up to three minutes, or you upload a vertical file you already have. On a computer, YouTube Studio takes it like any video: Create, then Upload videos, then a square or vertical file of three minutes or less. You can pick up to 15 at once, then finish each draft before it goes live.</p>

  <p>There's no Shorts switch anywhere in that flow. The file's shape and length decide.</p>

  <h2>How do Shorts thumbnails work?</h2>

  <p>You can pick a frame from the Short as its thumbnail, before or after upload, and add text and filters. A custom image is a desktop job.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Custom thumbnails for Shorts are currently only available to add in YouTube Studio on a computer. Your account must be verified to use this feature." Shorts thumbnails should be 9:16, 2160 x 3840 pixels, at least 640 pixels tall, JPG or PNG.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: YouTube Help, "Add custom thumbnails on YouTube," read October 2026, platform's own help text. https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/72431</p>

  <p>The path is Content, then Shorts, then the Short, then Upload file under Thumbnail. One warning from the same page: a vertical video given a 16:9 custom thumbnail gets that thumbnail replaced by an automatic 4:5 crop on the home, Explore and subscription pages, so the thumbnail wants the same tall frame as the video.</p>

