  <p>TikTok's own help page on making a post prints no length at all. It tells you to "select a maximum length" in the app. Its Camera tools page does give one: 10 minutes for a video recorded in TikTok, 60 for one uploaded. A clip that an app posts for you falls under the developer documentation's figure instead.</p>

  <p>Put the rows side by side and one file clears every limit on the table: 9:16, 1080 by 1920, under 60 seconds. It sits inside YouTube's three minutes, under the three-minute mark past which Instagram stops showing a reel to new audiences, inside the three minutes TikTok gives every creator, and it plays whole in a Shorts ad slot. Instagram also asks for at least 720 pixels and 30 frames per second, which that file meets.</p>

  <h2>Where does hubStudio fit?</h2>

  <p>Shorts autopilot, in the hubStudio app, starts from a long video: an interview, a talk, a webinar. It picks the moments that stand on their own and frames each at 9:16 around the speaker. Captions go on word by word, plus an optional hook line. Each short is saved in the Assets Library as an MP4 at 1080 x 1920, with a caption for each network you kept (YouTube Shorts, TikTok, Instagram Reels).</p>

  <p>You choose 3, 5, 8 or 10 shorts, at 15 to 30, 30 to 60 or 60 to 90 seconds. Pick the longest band and the Content ID rule above applies, so leave claimed music out.</p>

  <p>The YouTube module then carries the Short to YouTube. Pick the clip from the library, write the title (100 characters at most) and the description (5,000) with AI or by hand, and send it for approval. hubStudio doesn't upload it. YouTube keeps uploads from apps it hasn't audited private, so Publish interactively copies the title, opens YouTube Studio and downloads the video, and you publish from your own channel. <a href="/help/youtube">The YouTube help article</a> walks it press by press, including the Altered content question for realistic AI footage.</p>

  <p>The module's Channel tab prepares the channel itself: name and handle, description, keywords, links, a 2560 x 1440 banner and an 800 x 800 profile picture. The same clips go out to Instagram, and to TikTok (in Beta), from <a href="/app/publish">the app's publishing page</a>. For TikTok creative beyond the clip, see the <a href="/solutions/platforms/tiktok">TikTok platform page</a>; for Shorts made by our studio, the <a href="/services/design/short-video">short video design service</a>.</p>

